Design for Test (DFT) techniques—including scan insertion, ATPG, and memory BIST—are critical for identifying manufacturing defects in VLSI chips by adding test logic during design. Key concepts include scan chain architecture, fault modeling (stuck-at, transition), test compression, and JTAG, aimed at increasing test coverage and reducing costs. 
Key DFT Concepts & Tutorials
Introduction to DFT: Slideshare provides an overview of why DFT is essential, defining it as adding extra logic for post-production testing.
Scan Design & Insertion: This involves replacing standard flip-flops with scan flip-flops to make internal nodes controllable and observable. Key topics include Scan Design Rules, DRC checks, and inserting scan chains.
ATPG (Automatic Test Pattern Generation): Learn to generate, simulate, and apply test patterns to detect defects (stuck-at, transition faults).
Memory BIST (Built-In Self-Test): Essential for testing embedded memories by adding dedicated controllers for self-testing.
JTAG & Boundary Scan: Understand IEEE 1149.1 standards for testing interconnects between chips on a board.
Test Compression: Techniques to reduce test application time and data volume. 

